# Which AI agents this console asks.
#
#   <id> [key=value ...]
#
# One agent per line; file order sets display order. Blank lines and anything
# after a # are ignored. Comment a line out to stop asking that agent.
#
# Supported ids : claude, codex, cursor, grok
# Supported keys: model, tiers
#
# Effort: the Effort selector drives `claude --effort`, codex's
# model_reasoning_effort and `grok --reasoning-effort` directly. Grok has no tier
# above `high`, so xhigh and max clamp onto it. Cursor has no effort flag — its tier is part
# of the model name — so write {effort} in the model and the selected level is
# substituted. `tiers=` lists the levels that family actually has; anything
# higher clamps down to the top of that list.
#
# Cursor model names: `cursor-agent models`.
#
# Cross-review: after every agent answers, they all review each other, then one
# writes the final answer. Mark that one with `final=true`; otherwise the first
# available agent in this file does it.
#
# Web access is ON by default — every CLI blocks the network in print mode, so
# each is given the narrowest switch that allows search:
#   claude  --allowedTools WebSearch WebFetch   (only those two tools)
#   codex   -c tools.web_search=true
#   cursor  --force                             (stays in read-only ask mode)
#   grok    on by default                       (--disable-web-search turns it off)
# Set `web=false` on a line to keep that agent offline.
#
# Read fresh on every request, so edits take effect without restarting the dev
# server. If this file is missing, every supported agent is used.

claude final=true web=true
codex web=true
cursor model=gemini-3.7-flash-{effort} tiers=low,medium,high web=true
grok web=true
